definition:
**Tuple relational calculus (TRC)** is a declarative query language based on first-order logic. A query has the form

$$
\{\, t \mid P(t) \,\}
$$

and returns the set of all tuples $t$ for which the formula $P$ is true. Formulas are built from:

- **atoms**: $t \in R$ (tuple $t$ belongs to relation $R$), $t[A]\ \theta\ s[B]$ and $t[A]\ \theta\ c$, where $\theta$ is one of $=, \ne, <, \le, >, \ge$ and $c$ is a constant;
- **connectives** $\wedge$, $\vee$, $\neg$, $\Rightarrow$;
- **quantifiers** $\exists s \in R\,(\ldots)$, "there is a tuple $s$ in $R$ such that", and $\forall s \in R\,(\ldots)$, "every tuple $s$ in $R$ satisfies".

A variable is **bound** if a quantifier introduces it and **free** otherwise; in a query, $t$ is the only free variable. A query is **safe** if every tuple in its result is built from values that appear in the database or in the query. For example, $\{ t \mid \neg (t \in R) \}$ is unsafe: it describes infinitely many tuples.

A **B+ tree** is a balanced, multiway search tree used as a dynamic multilevel index. With the **order** $p$ defined as the maximum number of child pointers of an internal node:

- an internal node holds $q \le p$ child pointers and $q - 1$ search keys $K_1 < \dots < K_{q-1}$, and the subtree between $K_{i-1}$ and $K_i$ holds the keys in that range;
- **leaves** hold up to $L$ (key, record pointer) pairs and a pointer to the next leaf, so the leaves form a sorted linked list;
- every search key appears in a leaf; keys in internal nodes are only separators (copies);
- all leaves are at the same depth;
- every node except the root is at least half full: internal nodes have at least $\lceil p/2 \rceil$ children and leaves at least $\lceil L/2 \rceil$ keys. A root that is not a leaf needs only 2 children.

A **B-tree** stores each key exactly once, together with its record pointer, in whichever node (internal or leaf) it sits; there is no leaf chain.

Conventions differ between textbooks: the order may mean the maximum number of pointers, the maximum number of keys or a minimum degree, and a separator equal to a key may send it left or right. Always use the definition the question gives.
